// spec: VAR-006, DCL-007
//
// A REVERSE conformance test: it records what the checker does WRONG and passes
// while the fault persists.  It turns red when the program below starts
// running, which is the point -- a fix is as much a change to be noticed as a
// regression.
//
// This program is CORRECT.  [DCL-007] resolves a free name in a subprogram body
// when the body RUNS rather than where it is written, so Report may read a
// module-level constant declared below it, provided the call happens after that
// declaration -- and the call is the last line of the file.  Write a type on
// LIMIT and the program runs and prints 7.
//
// Without one it is refused: 'Expected Integer, found an untyped expression.'
// A declaration whose type is INFERRED is not recorded until the checker walks
// its declaration, so a body checked ABOVE it sees no type at all, and
// [VAR-006] -- "a value whose type could not be determined does not satisfy a
// written type" -- then refuses the assignment.
//
// ⚠️ [VAR-006] already names this class of fault and rejects it.  Its own note
// says refusing an untyped value at an assignment "refused correct code, and
// the wrong trade would have been to punish a program for the checker's blind
// spot", and lists three blind spots closed for that reason.  This is a fourth,
// and the same argument applies to it.
//
// ⚠️ It reaches this compiler's own sources.  ObjFunction.a24 declares EXACT,
// WIDENING and ABSORBING below Select, which reads them -- so writing the
// natural annotation on that loop, 'for var Pass : Integer := EXACT;', makes
// algc refuse the file it was built from.  The absence of a type is what keeps
// it compiling.

procedure Report ();
var
    Bound : Integer;

begin
    Bound := LIMIT;

    WriteLn (Bound);
end

const LIMIT := 7;

Report ();
